# Fleet-wide clone, structural verify, and secret purge

For auditing an entire GitHub account's worth of repos — dozens or hundreds,
only some of which are real services. The class-level lesson: **scale changes
what you can prove, so bound the scope explicitly and say what was not run.**

## 1. Bound the scope before cloning

A full account inventory is usually mostly game mods, experiments, and
abandoned prototypes. Cloning all of it is cheap (a few GB, minutes) but
reviewing it is not. Ask which repos count and *how deep* to go — structure
only, or install and run. "Rebuild everything" is not a scope.

Distinguish inventory from work. Keep a scratch clone area (`~/Projects/_inventory/`)
separate from the real working checkouts, or a read-only audit silently becomes
a live working tree.

## 2. Clone with full history if you will scan history

`--depth 1` makes a history scan impossible. Full clone is fine at this scale:

```python
ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=8)  # over subprocess.run(["git","clone",...])
GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0              # fail fast, never block on a credential
GIT_SSH_COMMAND="ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new"
```

Record a machine-readable result per repo. A clone loop that prints nothing on
success and nothing on failure leaves you unable to distinguish "all present"
from "half silently skipped" — count the successes explicitly against the
inventory size.

## 3. Classify before judging

Group by manifest-derived kind (node / python / c++ / static-web /
empty-or-docs). Syntax-checking a C++ mod tree as if it were Python, or
scanning `venv/`, buries real findings. Skip `.git`, `node_modules`, `venv`,
`__pycache__`, `dist`, `build` in every walk.

Empty repos are a finding, not noise: report "0 source files, N commits" so
the user sees the repo exists and is a stub.

## 4. A repo's own upstream is evidence

When a clone shows a parse error you already fixed in your working copy, diff
the two. It proves the fix was real **and** that it was never pushed — worth
stating explicitly, because "locally fixed" and "fixed everywhere" are
different states and the user may believe the second.

## 5. Secret scan: working tree AND history

Two different questions. The tree answers "what leaks if I push today"; history
answers "what already leaked and is still recoverable by anyone who ever
cloned". A secret deleted in a later commit is **not gone** — it stays in every
clone and in GitHub's fork/cache long after the file is deleted.

Report and change nothing by default. Rank by severity, and separate
*likely-live* from placeholder.

Placeholder filtering must not eat real findings. Self-test the scanner
against known-positive and known-negative strings before you believe its
report. Documented vendor examples (`AKIA...EXAMPLE`) are correctly filtered;
a live-shaped key must not be. A file named `keygen key.txt` may hold
`ssh-keygen` *console output* and no key material at all — read before
reporting.

## 6. Python regex trap when writing a scanner

`re` rejects `(?i)` anywhere but the start of the whole pattern, so a
multi-alternation filename pattern with a per-branch inline flag fails to
compile. Split into separate compiled patterns and pass `re.I` instead of
inlining the flag.

Verify with a compile-all loop plus positive/negative self-tests before
launching a long run — a crash 20 seconds in wastes the whole pass.

**Build the self-test from documented real formats, and count characters.**
A fixture that is one or two characters short of a pattern's minimum length
will not match, and it is tempting to blame the pattern. It proves nothing:
check the sample against the real format first. A bot token needs its full
post-colon length; a short sample "fails" a perfectly correct regex. Getting
this backwards means patching working detection logic to satisfy a broken
fixture — and shipping a regression to fix a non-bug.

A vendor's own documented example key (`[REDACTED-SECRET-2026-09-28]`) is correctly
suppressed as a placeholder. Keep both cases in the self-test so a change to
the placeholder filter cannot silently start eating live keys.

## 7. Purging a secret from history

Irreversible and SHA-changing. **Confirm before touching history, and confirm
the push separately** — a local rewrite purges nothing until force-pushed.
Bundle first so the rewrite is locally reversible:

```bash
git bundle create /tmp/pre-rewrite.bundle --all
uv tool install git-filter-repo   # or pipx; NOT pip on PEP 668 systems
git-filter-repo --replace-text <(echo 'LITERAL==>***REMOVED-TOKEN***') --force
```

`--force` is required outside a fresh clone, and it **removes the `origin`
remote** — note that, do not be surprised by it.

Then verify properly, because "it printed success" is not proof:

```bash
git log --all --oneline -S'<literal>' | wc -l          # expect 0
# and in EVERY blob, not just commit diffs:
git rev-list --all --objects | awk '{print $1}' | while read o; do
  [ "$(git cat-file -t $o)" = blob ] && git cat-file -p "$o" | grep -q '<literal>' && echo "FOUND $o"
done | wc -l
git rev-list --all --count                            # commits preserved?
```

Warn explicitly while the purge is un-pushed: **the old value is still
retrievable from the remote until you force-push.** A rotated server-side
token that the old value no longer authorizes is defence-in-depth, not
erasure.

## 8. History rewrite discards uncommitted work

`git-filter-repo` resets the working tree to the rewritten HEAD, silently
discarding uncommitted edits. Re-apply them afterward and re-verify — the
rewrite is not the last step.

## 9. Corrupted-but-committed files

A file that has never parsed in any commit is not a regression to fix, it is a
finding to report. Prove it per-commit (`git show <rev>:<file> | py_compile -`)
before deleting, and check the project's real entrypoint — a stub `main.py`
superseded by a TypeScript CLI in `src/` is dead weight, not the tool.

## 10. Committed dependency trees

`node_modules/` in git is both a size problem and a *security* one: it is how
a third-party library's placeholder example keys end up in your history, where
a history scanner will flag them forever. Untrack it, add `.gitignore`, and
expect the already-pushed blobs to need the history-purge path above.

A tracked `.env` is the same smell even when today's contents are harmless —
two `localhost` URLs in a committed file is a pattern that eventually receives
a real key. Report it as a finding regardless of current contents.
